---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Index from '../components/Index.astro';
import Hazard from '../components/Hazard.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';

const tiers = [
  {
    machine: 'A laptop with 8 GB of shared memory',
    size: 'Up to about 4 billion parameters',
    picks: 'Phi-3 Mini, Llama 3.2 3B',
    good: 'Summaries, tidying text, simple questions',
  },
  {
    machine: 'A laptop with 16 GB, or an M-series Mac',
    size: 'Up to about 9 billion',
    picks: 'Llama 3.1 8B, Qwen 2.5 7B, Mistral 7B, Gemma 2 9B',
    good: 'Most everyday writing and light code',
  },
  {
    machine: 'A desktop card with 24 GB',
    size: 'Up to about 32 billion, quantised',
    picks: 'Qwen 2.5 32B, a distilled reasoning model',
    good: 'Real code, longer documents',
  },
  {
    machine: 'Two cards, or 64 GB of unified memory',
    size: '70 billion, quantised',
    picks: 'Llama 3.3 70B',
    good: 'Close to a mid-sized hosted model, much slower',
  },
];

const tools = [
  { name: 'Ollama', what: 'Command line, one binary, pulls models by name', where: 'Mac, Windows, Linux' },
  { name: 'LM Studio', what: 'A window with a model picker and a chat', where: 'Mac, Windows, Linux' },
  { name: 'llama.cpp', what: 'The engine most of the others are built on', where: 'Anywhere that compiles' },
  { name: 'Jan', what: 'An open-source desktop app with a local server', where: 'Mac, Windows, Linux' },
];
---

<Base
  title="Running a model yourself"
  description="What a local model costs, when it is lighter than a hosted one and when it is not, and what your machine can actually hold."
  status="draft"
  here="/local-llms"
>
  <section class="band band--first">
    <h1 class="statement" style="font-size: var(--step-4); max-width: 20ch">
      Your laptop is a data centre with a bad cooling budget.
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Running a model yourself keeps your text on your own disk, which is a good reason on its own.
      Whether it uses less energy is a separate question, and the answer is often no.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={1} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">Why it can be lighter</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A small model does less arithmetic per token than a large one, by roughly the ratio of their
        active parameters. An 8 billion parameter model asked a simple question is doing a fraction
        of the work a frontier model does answering the same question, and most questions people ask
        do not need the frontier model.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is also no building around it. No power usage effectiveness multiplier of 1.1 or 1.2,
        no cooling towers evaporating water, no network. The only water is at the power station that
        charged your battery.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={2} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">Why it often is not</h2>
    </div>

    <Hazard label="The part people skip">
      <p>
        A data centre batches many requests onto one accelerator and keeps it saturated. Your
        machine runs one request at a time, and draws power for the whole time it is thinking,
        including the long stretches where it is waiting on memory rather than computing.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0">
        So a model that runs at four tokens a second on your laptop can cost more per token than the
        same class of model in a rack, even though the rack has a cooling bill. The{' '}
        <a href="/calculator">calculator</a> has a switch for this, and it will tell you when your
        own machine comes out worse.
      </p>
    </Hazard>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={3} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">What your machine can hold</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buai-scroll-x">
      <table class="buai-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Machine</th>
            <th scope="col">Model size</th>
            <th scope="col">Worth trying</th>
            <th scope="col">Good at</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            tiers.map((tier) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{tier.machine}</th>
                <td>{tier.size}</td>
                <td>{tier.picks}</td>
                <td>{tier.good}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="note" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
      Sizes assume four-bit quantisation, which is what nearly everyone runs locally and costs a
      little quality for a lot of memory.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={4} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">What to run it with</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="buai-scroll-x">
      <table class="buai-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Tool</th>
            <th scope="col">What it is</th>
            <th scope="col">Runs on</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            tools.map((tool) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{tool.name}</th>
                <td>{tool.what}</td>
                <td>{tool.where}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="note" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
      Any of them that serves a web interface on localhost can be watched by the extension, once you
      grant it that address. It asks rather than assuming.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <Faq>
      <details>
        <summary>How accurate is the local figure?</summary>
        <p>
          Less accurate than the hosted ones, and they are not very accurate either. It comes from a
          formula using active parameters and the efficiency of your accelerator class, not from a
          measurement of your machine. One case we checked against measured joules per token on a
          desktop card came out about a third low.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does the tool measure my actual power draw?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Reading a real power meter needs privileges a browser extension does not have and
          should not ask for. If you have a wall meter, use it, and please tell us what it says.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is a small hosted model better than a small local one?</summary>
        <p>
          For energy, usually yes, because of batching. For privacy, no. They are different
          questions and this project only answers the first one.
        </p>
      </details>
    </Faq>
  </section>
</Base>
